<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>. ValidationRule.Description is
        capped at 255 characters; a comment is repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata
        API. It must sit INSIDE the root element. A comment placed ABOVE <ValidationRule>
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent xml
        file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        ADDED 2026-08-20 (Tranche 2, Workstream A4). THE FIRST VALIDATION RULE ON THIS OBJECT,
        AND IT CARRIES THE SHARED RATIONALE FOR ALL FOUR. The siblings
        (Closing_Period_Days_Not_Negative, Due_Diligence_Days_Not_Negative,
        Final_Price_Required_Before_Executed) point here.
        =====================================================================================

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 LOI__c IS A DUAL RECORD TYPE OBJECT SERVING BOTH MODULES. EVERY RULE DEPLOYED HERE
        FIRES ON ACQUISITION LOIs TOO. THAT IS ASSESSED, NOT OVERLOOKED.
        =====================================================================================
        Acquisition_LOI runs Draft, Under Review, Submitted, Negotiation, Signed.
        Disposition_LOI runs Received, Under Review, Negotiation, Signed (narrowed 2026-08-21;
        was Received, Under Review, Countered by DPEG, Counter Received from Buyer, Executed -
        see that record type's own header).

        THIS RULE AND THE TWO DAY COUNT RULES ARE SAFE ON BOTH PATHS BECAUSE THEY ARE
        ARITHMETIC INVARIANTS, not workflow gates: a counter price of zero and a negative day
        count are wrong whether DPEG is buying or selling. They need no record type scoping
        and deliberately have none.
        Final_Price_Required_Before_Executed is the one rule here that IS path specific.
        🔴 AMENDED 2026-08-21: it no longer achieves that by naming a value - both record types'
        terminals are now 'Signed', so a value-named test can no longer distinguish them. It is
        scoped instead by a lookup discriminator, NOT(ISBLANK(Disposition__c)). See that rule's
        own header for the full retraction and reasoning; its API name is unchanged despite no
        longer literally matching 'Executed'.

        🔴 DO NOT ADD A RecordType.DeveloperName TEST TO ANY OF THESE FOUR "FOR SAFETY".
        Two in repo arguments say why: Broker_Lookup_Is_Off_Market_Only's Master record type
        argument (a record type test also blocks Master rows, which TestDataFactory produces
        deliberately when a record type is absent from a scratch org), and
        NdaSelector.queryExpiryAlerts's warning that a record type filter can ship a feature
        inert. A value named rule survives a record type rename; a record type test does not.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 NOT(ISBLANK(...)) COMES FIRST, AND IT IS LOAD BEARING, NOT PADDING.
        =====================================================================================
        A blank Counter_Price__c is the NORMAL state: most LOIs are never countered. On a
        Currency field ISBLANK is the only test that distinguishes an empty field from a
        deliberately entered 0, so without the guard this rule would refuse every uncountered
        LOI, which is nearly all of them.

        =====================================================================================
        NO ISCHANGED / ISNEW GUARD ON THIS RULE OR THE TWO DAY COUNT RULES. Each is an
        INVARIANT, not a transition, and each is SELF CLEARING: the remedy, correcting or
        clearing the offending number, is itself a permitted save, so an unguarded rule cannot
        trap a row. ⚠ SYSTEM_MODE DML does NOT bypass validation rules, so a bad row refuses
        machine writes too until it is corrected. That is intended, and it is why the remedy
        must stay permitted.
        (Final_Price_Required_Before_Executed DOES carry a guard, because it gates a
        TRANSITION rather than asserting an invariant. Different job, different shape.)
        =====================================================================================
        MEASURED IMPACT AT CREATION: ZERO ROWS REFUSED. Queried on usman-dpeg, 2026-08-20:
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM LOI__c                                            -> 11
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM LOI__c WHERE Counter_Price__c &lt;= 0             -> 0
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM LOI__c WHERE Closing_Period_Days__c &lt; 0
                                          OR Due_Diligence_Days__c &lt; 0         -> 0
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM LOI__c WHERE Stage__c = 'Executed'
                                          AND Final_Agreed_Price__c = null        -> 0
        Eleven LOIs exist and not one violates any of the four rules. 🔴 THAT MEASUREMENT
        AGES. Re-run all four at deploy time rather than citing this line; a non zero result
        freezes a live LOI until someone corrects it, which is an escalation, not a deploy.
    -->
    <fullName>Counter_Price_Is_Positive</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>Blocks a zero or negative Counter Price on an LOI. A blank counter is legitimate and is left alone; only a counter that has been entered is checked. An arithmetic invariant, so it is deliberately unscoped and fires on both record types.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>AND(
    NOT(ISBLANK(Counter_Price__c)),
    Counter_Price__c &lt;= 0
)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorMessage>Counter Price must be greater than zero. Leave it blank if this LOI has not been countered; enter the countered amount if it has.</errorMessage>
    <errorDisplayField>Counter_Price__c</errorDisplayField>
</ValidationRule>
